| **Database scanning** | Confirm whether Wiz provides database-engine vulnerability assessment or primarily cloud configuration and workload vulnerability findings for each database service. |
| **Web application scanning** | Confirm whether Wiz is being used for application dependency vulnerability assessment, web application exposure analysis, or additional application security capabilities. Do not assume DAST coverage. |
| **CIS Level 2 scanning** | Confirm the exact CIS benchmarks, versions, supported resources, and Level 2 checks enabled in Wiz. |
| **Report generation** | Confirm the actual Wiz navigation paths, export capabilities, available report fields, and authorization boundary filtering mechanisms. |

## Final Recommendation

For your GovRAMP Moderate Phase 3 activities, I recommend maintaining these eight procedures as separate sections within a single **Wiz Security Scanning, Compliance Monitoring, and POA&M Reporting Standard Operating Procedure (SOP)**.

The procedures should share a common reporting cadence, authorization boundary definition, evidence retention process, and POA&M reconciliation methodology.

Most importantly, distinguish between **vulnerability scanning, cloud configuration assessment, software inventory, and application security testing**. Although Wiz can provide findings across several of these areas, the underlying assessment methods and coverage are not necessarily equivalent.

This distinction will help ensure that your GovRAMP documentation accurately represents the security assessments being performed and that unsupported scanning capabilities are not inadvertently claimed.
